# .github/CODEOWNERS (Security; SPEC/00 §5's Security row, ADR-0003
# amendment 2). The SPEC/00 §5 seat table as owner lines, from M02 PR 2.
#
# How it is read. GitHub reads the login and uses this file for review
# routing only: `require_code_owner_review` is false on the main ruleset
# and stays false (R1: no gate waits for a human). The gates read the
# seat, this way: `src/gates/` takes the `# seat:` line above each block as the
# owner of every pattern below it, reads this file FROM THE BASE REF (SPEC/02 §6),
# and refuses a PR that changes a path with no ruling from that seat.
# `make validate` checks that every tracked file matches a line, that no
# file matches lines from two seats, that every `.claude/agents/<name>.md`
# is under the seat its own front matter names, and that each login is a
# real GitHub user.
#
# At M02 every seat is one person (R1), so every line names the same
# login. A path not listed here has no seat, and a file no seat owns is
# deleted, not adopted (SPEC/00 §5).
#
# A manifest is one file with several owners (ADR-0003 amendment 1): the
# file is Engineering's here, and `src/gates/` attributes each changed
# field to its owner (model and budgets: Threshold Owner; guardrail: Rule
# Owner; seats and endpoint_allowlist: Security; may_call, may_be_called_by,
# ceilings, version: Tool Owner). GitHub cannot express that; the gate does.

